======================================================================================================================
R3: the shared gates as statements (G1 is H1; the door lives or dies on G1)
======================================================================================================================
  G1 target        : PASS (H1 above).
  G2 CMB/growth    : the cold fluid is CDM (collisionless, cold shells under Newtonian gravity only), so its linear growth and
                     CMB behaviour are LCDM's by construction (not re-tested here).  The simulation's idealised background
                     (baryons outside the core held smooth) slows the cold perturbation's growth a little; that is a set-up
                     simplification, not a property of the mechanism.
  G3 reciprocity   : ordinary Newtonian gravity between the cold fluid and the baryons: the reaction on the baryons is the
                     Newtonian pull of the cold mass (third law) and the dynamics conserve energy.  The core is held static,
                     which is an untested hypothesis, not a result.
  G4 constants     : no constant in the dynamics beyond LCDM's.  The result depends on declared initial-condition choices
                     (z_i = 100, the full core present from z_i, the bracket q, the smooth-background reading); a pass that
                     needed one of them would count it against G4.  kappa enters only through the target's a0.
  G5 well-posedness: collisionless CDM (Vlasov-Poisson) is well posed; Newtonian gravity is Solar-System (Cassini) safe.

  kappa = 1/2 and Omega_c h^2 stay fitted. Nothing here says the data favour either model, or that the theory is closed.

  6/9 checks pass; load-bearing failures: 1   (22 s)
